        "Parallel plate or cone-and-plate measuring geometries suited for polymer melts in the Environmental Test Chamber"
      ]
    },
    {
      "instrument_id": "EQ18",
      "role": "supplementary",
      "technique_id": "differential_scanning_calorimetry",
      "property_id": "melting_temperature",
      "technique": "Differential scanning calorimetry (DSC)",
      "why": "It identifies thermal transitions to confirm whether the extrusion temperature is above the complete melting endotherm or if un-melted crystallites or exothermic reactions (e.g., crosslinking) are present.",
      "accessories_to_confirm": [
        "Suitable high-temperature sample pans and lids",
        "Cooling accessory for controlled heating/cooling runs"
      ]
    },
    {
      "instrument_id": "EQ17",
      "role": "supplementary",
      "technique_id": "thermogravimetric_analysis",
      "property_id": "thermal_stability",
      "technique": "Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA)",
      "why": "It checks the thermal stability of the polymer at extrusion temperatures to see if volatilization or decomposition is occurring.",
      "accessories_to_confirm": [
        "Sample pans (platinum, ceramic, or aluminium)",
        "Purge gas supply (nitrogen)"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "not_available": [
    {
      "technique_id": "capillary_rheometry",
      "technique": "Capillary rheometry",
      "why_useful": "Extrusion processes typically generate high shear rates (100 to >10,000 1/s) that exceed rotational rheometers; capillary rheometry measures viscosity under realistic extrusion flow conditions."
    },
    {
      "technique_id": "gel_permeation_chromatography",
      "technique": "Gel permeation chromatography (GPC/SEC)",
      "why_useful": "High viscosity often stems from unexpectedly high molecular weight or polymer branching, which GPC determines by measuring the molecular weight distribution."
    }
  ],
  "clarifying_question": null,
  "summary": "You can measure the melt viscosity of your polymer across temperature and shear rate ranges using the Discovery HR20 rheometer equipped with the Environmental Test Chamber (EQ21). In addition, differential scanning calorimetry (EQ18) can determine if the material has completely melted or if crosslinking occurs at your extrusion temperature. Note that true extrusion shear rates often require a capillary rheometer, which is not available in the listed facility."
}

{
